+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+
+Virtio-msg Linux Bridge
+=======================
+
+This document describes how to use the Linux bridge backend for
+``virtio-msg`` from the QEMU command line.
+
+Overview
+--------
+
+The Linux bridge backend connects a QEMU ``virtio-msg`` transport to a host
+bridge endpoint (typically through ``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``).
+
+Most users should instantiate the transport container device:
+
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport``
+
+This container model creates:
+
+- a top-level ``virtio-msg-outer-bus``
+- a ``virtio-msg`` transport
+- an internal ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge`` backend
+
+A standalone backend model is also available for integrators that already
+instantiate ``virtio-msg`` separately:
+
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge``
+
+Requirements
+------------
+
+- QEMU built with ``CONFIG_VIRTIO_MSG_LINUX_BRIDGE``.
+- Host bridge device node available (default: ``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``).
+- A valid bridge endpoint.
+
+Endpoint Syntax
+---------------
+
+The ``endpoint`` property format is ``bus-name[:bus-id]``:
+
+- ``bus-name`` is required.
+- ``bus-id`` is optional.
+- ``bus-id`` is passed as a string to the kernel bridge backend.
+
+Examples:
+
+- ``loopback``
+- ``ffa:5``
+- ``ffa:0x5``
+
+Canonical Transport Mode
+------------------------
+
+Use ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport`` as the default entry point:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -device virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport,id=vmsgt0,endpoint=loopback \
+   -device virtio-rng-device,bus=/vmsgt0/bus/virtio-msg/bus0/virtio-msg-dev
+
+To use a non-default bridge node:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -device virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport,id=vmsgt0,endpoint=ffa:5,devnode=/dev/virtio-msg-bridge0
+
+Standalone Backend Mode
+-----------------------
+
+Standalone mode is intended for integration with an already existing
+``virtio-msg`` object:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -device virtio-msg,id=vmsg0 \
+   -device virtio-msg-linux-bridge,id=vmsgbr0,endpoint=loopback,bus=/vmsg0/msg-bus \
+   -device virtio-rng-device,bus=/vmsg0/bus0/virtio-msg-dev
+
+Use standalone mode only when you need explicit control over how
+``virtio-msg`` is instantiated.
+
+Properties
+----------
+
+The ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport`` device exposes these properties:
+
+- ``endpoint`` (required)
+- ``create-endpoint`` (optional, default ``off``)
+- ``devnode`` (optional, default ``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``)
+- ``tx-ring-entries`` (default ``256``)
+- ``tx-ring-entry-size`` (default ``128`` in transport mode)
+- ``rx-ring-entries`` (default ``256``)
+- ``rx-ring-entry-size`` (default ``128`` in transport mode)
+
+The standalone ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge`` backend exposes these properties:
+
+- ``endpoint`` (required)
+- ``create-endpoint`` (optional, default ``off``)
+- ``devnode`` (optional, default ``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``)
+- ``tx-ring-entries`` (default ``256``)
+- ``tx-ring-entry-size`` (default ``128``)
+- ``rx-ring-entries`` (default ``256``)
+- ``rx-ring-entry-size`` (default ``128``)
+- ``vmm-max-msg-size`` (optional, default ``128``)
+
+``create-endpoint=off`` keeps launch policy fail-fast if the selected endpoint
+does not already exist in the host bridge backend.
+Use ``create-endpoint=on`` only when you explicitly want QEMU to create a
+missing endpoint (for example, offline staging workflows).
+
+QEMU uses the opened bridge ``devnode`` file descriptor for the retained
+synchronous operations only: ``RESOLVE_ENDPOINT``, ``RELEASE_ENDPOINT``,
+``ATTACH``, ``DETACH``, and ``mmap()`` of DMA-visible windows. Runtime endpoint
+lifecycle, topology, map, and error control is carried as bridge-local messages
+on the shared TX/RX rings. Kernel-to-QEMU runtime progress is signaled through
+the attach-time ``call_fd`` wakeup, not through runtime readiness on the bridge
+device file descriptor.
+
+In transport mode, the bridge is attached with ``vmm_max_msg_size=128``, and
+the default ring geometry is 256 entries by 128-byte slots for both TX and RX.
+For the current bridge ring UAPI, transport-mode ring geometry is effectively
+fixed to that 256x128 shape rather than a general tuning knob.
+Endpoint ``max_msg_size`` is still learned per attach epoch from ``ATTACH``
+and later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` lifecycle refresh events, and may be smaller.
+The online ``caps.max_msg_size`` value is the negotiated maximum raw frame size
+for both transport-class ``virtio-msg`` frames and bridge-local bus frames.
+The effective transport maximum message size is the smaller of
+``vmm-max-msg-size`` and the current endpoint ``max_msg_size`` when an endpoint
+maximum is known. QEMU accepts only v2-compliant maximum message sizes in the
+``52..128`` byte range; a reported value of ``0`` is treated as ``128``.
+For standalone mode, both rings must use exactly ``256`` entries, and
+``entry-size`` must equal the attach-time ``vmm-max-msg-size`` rounded up to
+the bridge alignment (``128`` bytes in the current UAPI).
+
+Operational Notes
+-----------------
+
+- Attach may succeed while the endpoint is offline.
+- ``ATTACH`` is the authoritative initial capability snapshot for one attach
+  epoch. It provides the transport revision and feature bits, plus endpoint
+  online/offline state, initial endpoint max message size, and
+  ``caps.online_epoch``.
+- When the attach snapshot is online, ``caps.online_epoch`` is non-zero and
+  identifies the initial online epoch. When the attach snapshot is offline,
+  ``caps.online_epoch`` is ``0`` and endpoint-scoped runtime work remains
+  blocked until a later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` ring event.
+- The in-tree bridge targets mandatory v2 transport behavior with transport
+  revision ``1``. Optional strict config-generation tracking is not supported
+  in this phase; QEMU clears ``VIRTIO_MSG_F_STRICT_CONFIG_GENERATION`` from
+  the effective transport feature mask.
+- Command-line startup prepares the bridge resources during realize, but the
+  first host ``ATTACH`` is issued from reset exit after QEMU's initial cold
+  reset. This avoids exposing a short attach epoch that is immediately reset
+  away before the machine starts running.
+- If attach already reports the endpoint online, QEMU uses that snapshot
+  directly, publishes topology for ``caps.online_epoch``, and does not wait for
+  a synthetic ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` event.
+- Later device or machine resets detach the current host bridge session, clear
+  local queue and map state, then re-attach to refresh the attach snapshot and
+  endpoint state.
+- If reset re-attach fails, QEMU reports the failure and leaves the transport
+  offline with no active host bridge session. Endpoint-scoped traffic and DMA
+  maps remain unavailable until a later reset re-attaches successfully or the
+  backend is recreated.
+- ``RESOLVE_ENDPOINT`` uses create flags only when ``create-endpoint=on``.
+- Default policy is ``create-endpoint=off`` for explicit pre-provisioned
+  endpoints.
+- QEMU keeps bridge-local RX processing active while an attach is offline or
+  waiting for topology publication, but it blocks endpoint-scoped RX, TX,
+  map-request, and pending required-delivery flush work until topology
+  publication succeeds for the current online epoch.
+- QEMU publishes a full topology snapshot through bridge-local
+  ``TOPOLOGY_RESET``, ``TOPOLOGY_DEV_ADD``, and ``TOPOLOGY_COMMIT`` TX ring
+  messages after the bridge is attached and machine initialization is complete
+  and the attach snapshot is online. It republishes the full snapshot after
+  each later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` transition.
+- The published transport topology uses external device numbers
+  ``[0..VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_DEVS - 1]``.
+- Runtime child hotplug and unplug are unsupported.
+- The ``virtio-msg`` proxy bus is intentionally not hotpluggable.
+- While offline or still waiting for topology publication to complete for a
+  new online epoch, transmit requests return retry and required-delivery TX is
+  not queued.
+- Required traffic (synchronous responses plus runtime notifications) is
+  queued only after topology publication succeeds for the current online epoch
+  when transient backpressure occurs. Delivery uses a bounded timer-backed
+  retry with 1 ms minimum delay, 200 ms maximum delay, and 8 failed retries
+  before the queued message expires.
+- Expired required response and runtime-notification messages fail the bridge
+  attach because the current bridge UAPI has no per-message delivery-failure
+  report for those traffic classes.
+- Required traffic retry is canceled when the queue drains or the endpoint
+  leaves the active online state.
+- Generic DMA windows are published and revoked by bridge-local ``MAP_ADD`` and
+  ``MAP_DEL`` request/response messages. Each accepted ``MAP_ADD`` creates one
+  pointer-backed RAM subregion in the private bridge DMA address space, owned
+  by a per-map QOM object.
+- The private bridge DMA address space is direct-map-only; bridge ``MAP_ADD``
+  subregions are the only DMA-visible RAM windows in that address space.
+- The transport uses this private DMA address space, so child virtio devices
+  must use transport DMA translation for queue and buffer accesses.
+- DMA visibility comes from those bridge map messages rather than from any
+  ``GET_SHM`` transport message path.
+- ``MAP_ADD`` requests may advertise ``mmap_length`` larger than the active DMA
+  window; QEMU maps the delivered extent with ``mmap()`` on the attached bridge
+  file descriptor, exposes exactly ``length`` bytes of guest-visible DMA space,
+  and copies the Linux request token into the matching ``MAP_ADD`` response.
+- ``MAP_DEL`` requests are revoke-first: QEMU removes DMA AddressSpace
+  visibility so no new DMA translations can be created for that publication
+  instance, then copies the Linux request token into the matching ``MAP_DEL``
+  response. The response is not proof that the final owner reference has
+  dropped or that QEMU has already called ``munmap()``.
+- ``RETENTION_REQUESTED`` on ``MAP_ADD`` is a hint that retaining an idle map
+  may avoid later setup cost. It protects the mapping from ordinary proactive
+  reclaim, but explicit ``MAP_DEL`` and exceptional cleanup paths such as
+  reset, detach, teardown, fault handling, or exceptional pressure may still
+  release it.
+- QEMU-initiated ``MAP_RELEASED`` events are delayed until DMA visibility has
+  been revoked and the final map owner release has performed the final
+  ``munmap()``. The normal trigger is bridge-local reclaim of non-retained
+  temporary mappings after an actual ``EVENT_USED`` frame has been committed
+  to the TX ring. Each reclaim pass releases at most one non-retained mapping,
+  choosing the oldest eligible map. Count and byte pressure thresholds of 256
+  live non-retained maps or 64 MiB of non-retained mapped bytes, plus reset
+  and teardown, remain additional bounds on non-retained map lifetime.
+- Retained mappings are released only for fault, reset, detach, teardown,
+  exceptional pressure, or other exceptional cleanup paths; a retained active
+  release removes the affected virtio device on the Linux side.
+- If QEMU sends ``MAP_RELEASED`` before Linux's matching ``MAP_DEL`` arrives,
+  QEMU keeps a tombstone so the crossing ``MAP_DEL`` can retire the released
+  record with a successful response.
+- QEMU does not release installed mappings only to free cache space.
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` is an attached-but-offline transition, not a detach.
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` clears pending required-delivery TX state, clears
+  pending map operation state, revokes all live bridge DMA mappings, and resets
+  attached child devices. The offline epoch is ``0``, and QEMU drops stale
+  old-epoch runtime state.
+- The child-device reset on ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` is retained QEMU transport
+  policy, not a Linux bridge ABI requirement.
+- Guest queue accesses and later DMA buffers use the bridge-managed DMA
+  address space, so map-message updates directly control when published DMA
+  ranges become usable or removable.
+- Runtime notification messages (``EVENT_CONFIG``, ``EVENT_AVAIL``, and
+  ``EVENT_USED``) are authoritative only when delivered through the
+  ``virtio-msg`` transport path. QEMU forwards those messages and does not
+  synthesize duplicate transport-visible notifications from host wakeups.
+- Out-of-band bridge signaling is treated only as a wakeup hint for polling
+  bridge state. It is not a second semantic occurrence of a runtime
+  notification.
+- Polling-only operation remains transparent: if no runtime notification
+  message is delivered, polling activity alone does not create a
+  transport-visible ``EVENT_AVAIL`` or ``EVENT_USED`` occurrence.
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` and ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` are the only runtime endpoint
+  lifecycle notifications.
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` is a lifecycle refresh payload carrying
+  ``online_epoch``, ``bus_name``, endpoint ``max_msg_size``, and ``reserved``
+  only; it does not republish revision or feature bitmaps.
+- Stale bridge-local messages for an older ``online_epoch`` are ignored.
+- Runtime bridge events and map operations are delivered on the RX ring and
+  discovered through ``call_fd`` wakeups; runtime ``dev_fd`` readiness is not a
+  second control channel.
+- ``ATTACH`` validates revision and initial max message size.
+- Runtime ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` validates endpoint ``max_msg_size`` only, using
+  ``0 -> VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE`` with
+  ``52 <= max_msg_size <= 128``.
+- Bridge map requests accept ``bus_addr == 0`` as long as length and alignment
+  requirements are satisfied.
+- If QEMU consumes a transport request that expected a response but cannot
+  produce a valid transport response, it emits ``BRIDGE_ERROR`` on the TX ring.
+  The error payload echoes the original transport ``dev_num`` and ``msg_id``
+  and carries a signed negative Linux errno; no additional transport response
+  follows for that request.
+
+Limitations
+-----------
+
+- Migration is blocked for this backend (bridge session state is host-local).
+- Optional ``VIRTIO_MSG_F_STRICT_CONFIG_GENERATION`` and
+  ``VIRTIO_F_NOTIF_CONFIG_DATA`` support is intentionally disabled in this
+  phase.
+- Notification-data feature support is disabled because the current bridge
+  event encoding does not carry notification-data semantics.
+
+Troubleshooting
+---------------
+
+- Confirm the bridge device node exists and permissions are correct.
+- Confirm the selected endpoint exists and is valid for the backend bus.
+- If the endpoint is offline, expect retry behavior until an online transition.
+- If transport devices do not appear after boot, confirm machine
+  initialization completed and the endpoint reached the online state so QEMU
+  could publish the topology snapshot on the TX ring and move the session into
+  its active online state.
+- If online events are rejected, verify bridge revision and max message size
+  match the supported capability contract.
+
+Tracing
+-------
+
+QEMU tracepoints are available for the Linux bridge backend. To enable all
+bridge trace events:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -trace enable=virtio_msg_linux_bridge_*
+
+To discover event names:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -trace help
+
+and filter for ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_``.
+
+To write traces to a file with the ``simple`` trace backend:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -trace enable=virtio_msg_linux_bridge_*,file=/tmp/vmsg.trace
+
+If QEMU is built without ``simple`` backend support, traces can still be
+captured by redirecting stderr.
+
+Developer Notes
+---------------
+
+Implementation details and internal contracts are documented in QEMU's
+developer documentation.
